# GitDATA guides. GitHub enforces. This file is the enforcement half — the project dogfooding
# its own law (north star #5: permissions via GitHub's out-of-the-box features).
#
# CODEOWNERS only binds once a ruleset on main requires code-owner review. Repo settings, not
# files, flip that switch — and it is now flipped, so every line below is live.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO `* @owner` CATCH-ALL. Do not add one back.
#
# A catch-all makes the sole code owner the only approver for EVERY pull request. Two things then
# follow, and the second one is the trap:
#
#   1. A review bot can no longer clear a PR. Its approval still counts toward "Required
#      approvals", but code-owner review is a SEPARATE gate that a non-owner cannot satisfy.
#   2. GitHub forbids approving your own pull request. On a solo repo the sole code owner is also
#      the author of nearly every PR — so the gate is unsatisfiable BY THE ONLY PERSON WHO COULD
#      SATISFY IT. Every PR is then permanently blocked, or permanently admin-bypassed. Neither
#      is a gate; one is a wall and the other is theatre.
#
# So ownership here is NARROW on purpose: it names the handful of files where a human decision is
# the point, and stays silent everywhere else. Routine work is cleared by review; the files below
# are not routine.
#
# Adding a path here has a real cost — it makes that path unmergeable without the owner, including
# when the owner wrote it. That cost is worth paying for a document that governs how the project
# thinks. It is not worth paying for code, which is what tests and review are for.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

